\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Eigenvalues of the generalized problem $(L,D)$ before shifting (left) versus eigenvalues of the shifted Laplacian ${\mathcal  L}_\sigma $ after shifting (right), computed on a 15-vertex illustrative graph. The unshifted problem has one eigenvalue at exactly zero, corresponding to graph connectivity; this is what makes $L$ singular. Adding $\sigma ^2\lambda _{\min }D$ shifts every eigenvalue upward by the same relative amount, eliminating the zero eigenvalue and making ${\mathcal  L}_\sigma $ symmetric positive definite.}}{9}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:phase2_eigenvalues}{{3}{9}{Eigenvalues of the generalized problem $(L,D)$ before shifting (left) versus eigenvalues of the shifted Laplacian $\L _\sigma $ after shifting (right), computed on a 15-vertex illustrative graph. The unshifted problem has one eigenvalue at exactly zero, corresponding to graph connectivity; this is what makes $L$ singular. Adding $\sigma ^2\lambda _{\min }D$ shifts every eigenvalue upward by the same relative amount, eliminating the zero eigenvalue and making $\L _\sigma $ symmetric positive definite}{figure.caption.3}{}}
